globalThis.COURSE_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"pageId":"assignments/example-homework-writeup","pageTitle":"Example Homework Write-Ups","heading":"Correct but incomplete solutions","anchor":"correct-but-incomplete-solutions","text":"Correct but incomplete solutions The calculations and final answers below are correct, but these solutions would not earn full credit."},{"pageId":"assignments/example-homework-writeup","pageTitle":"Example Homework Write-Ups","heading":"Example assignment","anchor":"example-assignment","text":"Example assignment"},{"pageId":"assignments/example-homework-writeup","pageTitle":"Example Homework Write-Ups","heading":"Example Homework Write-Ups","anchor":"example-homework-write-ups","text":"Example Homework Write-Ups This page shows what a complete handwritten homework solution should look like. The goal is not a long proof. For each exercise, write at least one sentence that explains your approach, and give word labels to the quantities in your calculation. The model solutions are set in larger, handwriting-like type to suggest the amount of work that you could reasonably write by hand."},{"pageId":"assignments/example-homework-writeup","pageTitle":"Example Homework Write-Ups","heading":"Exercise 1","anchor":"exercise-1","text":"Exercise 1 Approach. Choose the two officers first and then choose the three ordinary representatives from the students who remain. Phase 1 — officers. If the president is a mathematics major, there are choices; reversing the majors gives choices. Thus the officers can be chosen in ways. Phase 2 — ordinary representatives. After choosing the officers, 16 students remain, so the three unordered representatives can be chosen in ways. Every council is obtained once by these two phases, so the product rule gives"},{"pageId":"assignments/example-homework-writeup","pageTitle":"Example Homework Write-Ups","heading":"Exercise 1","anchor":"exercise-1","text":"Exercise 1 This is incomplete because it does not say what is chosen in each phase, what the two officer terms count, or why the two main quantities are multiplied."},{"pageId":"assignments/example-homework-writeup","pageTitle":"Example Homework Write-Ups","heading":"Exercise 1: Forming a student council","anchor":"exercise-1-forming-a-student-council","text":"Exercise 1: Forming a student council Points: 10 A student organization has 10 mathematics majors and 8 computer science majors. It will choose one president, one secretary, and three ordinary representatives. No student may hold two positions. The president and secretary must have different majors. The three ordinary-representative positions are not distinguished, and those representatives may have either major. How many councils are possible?"},{"pageId":"assignments/example-homework-writeup","pageTitle":"Example Homework Write-Ups","heading":"Exercise 2","anchor":"exercise-2","text":"Exercise 2 Approach. Count all seven-digit integers and subtract the ones whose digits are all different. Universe — all seven-digit integers. The first digit has 9 choices and each other digit has 10 choices, so Forbidden — no digit repeats. The first digit has 9 choices. The remaining digits then have choices in order, so Therefore the difference rule gives"},{"pageId":"assignments/example-homework-writeup","pageTitle":"Example Homework Write-Ups","heading":"Exercise 2","anchor":"exercise-2","text":"Exercise 2 Approach. Count all seven-digit integers and subtract the ones whose digits are all different. Universe — all seven-digit integers. There are such integers. Forbidden — no digit repeats. The first digit has 9 choices. The remaining digits then have choices in order, so Therefore This solution has a useful approach sentence and identifies both sets. It is still incomplete because it merely states without explaining why the first factor is 9 or what the exponent 6 represents. The first incomplete example gives only a formula. The second is much closer to complete, but it leaves one important part of its formula unexplained. Both show that the words must account for the whole model, not merely appear somewhere in the solution. The labels do not have to match these examples exactly. They may be written beside a formula or on separate lines, and abbreviations are fine after they are defined. What matters is that a reader can tell what each important number counts and why the displayed operations match the approach."},{"pageId":"assignments/example-homework-writeup","pageTitle":"Example Homework Write-Ups","heading":"Exercise 2: Seven-digit numbers with repetition","anchor":"exercise-2-seven-digit-numbers-with-repetition","text":"Exercise 2: Seven-digit numbers with repetition Points: 10 How many seven-digit positive integers have at least one repeated digit? Recall that a seven-digit integer cannot begin with 0."},{"pageId":"assignments/example-homework-writeup","pageTitle":"Example Homework Write-Ups","heading":"Full-credit solutions","anchor":"full-credit-solutions","text":"Full-credit solutions These solutions include the required words but are intentionally concise."},{"pageId":"assignments/example-homework-writeup","pageTitle":"Example Homework Write-Ups","heading":"Sample Rubric","anchor":"sample-rubric","text":"Sample Rubric Each exercise is graded out of 10 points. 2 points: approach and modeling. At least one sentence explains what is being counted and why the chosen rule applies. 2 points: intermediate quantities and factors. One point is for giving the important quantities word labels and meanings, such as Phase 1 — officers and Phase 2 — representatives, or Universe — all seven-digit integers and Forbidden — no digit repeats. One point is for explaining any non-obvious terms or factors in those quantities. 4 points: intermediate counts. The two main quantities are counted correctly, worth 2 points each. 1 point: combination. The quantities are combined correctly using the product, sum, or difference rule. 1 point: final answer. The numerical answer is clearly stated. The full-credit models receive 10 out of 10. The incomplete solution to Exercise 1 receives 6 out of 10: it earns the calculation, combination, and final-answer points, but none of the communication points. The incomplete solution to Exercise 2 receives 9 out of 10: its approach and labels are clear, but it loses one point because the factors counting are not explained. A correct bare formula is therefore not a full-credit solution. On the other hand, no long explanation is required: one useful approach sentence plus short word labels for the main quantities is enough. Equivalent labels and wording are welcome; students do not need to copy the exact format above."},{"pageId":"assignments/hw01","pageTitle":"Homework 1","heading":"Digits on a pentagon, without repetition","anchor":"digits-on-a-pentagon-without-repetition","text":"Digits on a pentagon, without repetition Points: 12 Place five distinct digits chosen from at the vertices of a regular pentagon. Two patterns are considered the same when one can be obtained from the other by rotating the pentagon. Reflections are not considered the same. The examples below illustrate this convention. The labels name the fixed vertex positions , beginning at the top vertex and proceeding clockwise. Pentagon with digits 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 clockwise from the top vertex. A rotation of Pattern 1. A nonrotation of Pattern 1 using digits 1, 2, 3, 5, 4. A pattern using digits 1, 2, 4, 6, 8. Patterns 1 and 2 are considered the same because a rotation carries one to the other. No other pair among the four displayed patterns is considered the same. How many different patterns are there? Need a free hint? First treat as fixed, distinguishable positions and count the assignments of five distinct digits. Next determine how many labeled arrangements represent each pattern up to rotation, explaining why the ``no repetition’’ condition makes this number the same for every pattern. Finally apply the division rule and simplify to a numerical answer. Pentagon with digits 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 clockwise from the top vertex. A rotation of Pattern 1, with digits 5, 1, 2, 3, 4 clockwise from the top vertex. Pentagon with digits 1, 2, 3, 5, 4 clockwise from the top vertex. Pentagon with digits 1, 2, 4, 6, 8 clockwise from the top vertex."},{"pageId":"assignments/hw01","pageTitle":"Homework 1","heading":"Forming a committee","anchor":"forming-a-committee","text":"Forming a committee Points: 10 A company has 20 employees in Marketing and 10 employees in Research and Development (R&D). It must form a committee with the following five positions: one chair; one vice-chair; and three ordinary members, whose positions are not distinguished from one another. No employee may hold more than one position. The chair and vice-chair must come from different departments; the ordinary members may come from either department. How many committees are possible? Need a free hint? Split into cases according to which department provides the chair and which provides the vice-chair. Choose the officers first, then the three ordinary members; remember that the ordinary-member positions are not distinguished."},{"pageId":"assignments/hw01","pageTitle":"Homework 1","heading":"Homework 1","anchor":"homework-1","text":"Homework 1 Due: Thursday, August 27, 2026 23:58Submit: Gradescope, through Blackboard’s assignment area. Please write your homework by hand. Start each exercise on a new page, scan your work to a legible PDF, and clearly mark the pages associated with every exercise."},{"pageId":"assignments/hw01","pageTitle":"Homework 1","heading":"Using every letter","anchor":"using-every-letter","text":"Using every letter Points: 10 How many strings of length 10 over the alphabet contain each of the three letters at least once? Need a free hint? Count all strings, then subtract the forbidden strings. A forbidden string uses exactly one or exactly two different letters. How many use only , only , or only ? For each of the pairs , , and , how many strings use both letters in the pair and no third letter?"},{"pageId":"assignments/hw01","pageTitle":"Homework 1","heading":"Why division by five can fail","anchor":"why-division-by-five-can-fail","text":"Why division by five can fail Points: 8 Now change Exercise 3 by allowing digits to repeat. Thus each of the five vertices may receive any digit from , while patterns that differ only by a rotation are still considered the same. A tempting argument is: There are assignments to five fixed vertices, and every pattern is represented five times by rotation, so the answer is . Explain carefully why this argument is incorrect. You are not being asked to find the correct total. Need a free hint? Explain why is the correct number of assignments when the vertices are fixed and labeled. Then list the distinct rotations represented by each of the clockwise strings 00000, 00001, and 01234, beginning at vertex , and use them to identify which requirement of the division rule fails. Focus on whether every pattern is counted the same number of times."},{"pageId":"assignments/index","pageTitle":"Assignments","heading":"Academic integrity, collaboration, and artificial intelligence","anchor":"academic-integrity-collaboration-and-artificial-intelligence","text":"Academic integrity, collaboration, and artificial intelligence You are encouraged to work with classmates, tutors, and me: talking through ideas, definitions, and problem-solving strategies is a valuable part of learning. You must understand every solution you submit and write it based on your own understanding. Please do not copy another person’s solution or write-up. You may use generative artificial intelligence as a study aid, but not to produce any work that you submit for credit. It is fine to ask a study question such as, ``I do not understand when to use generating functions to count combinations with restrictions.’’ It is not acceptable to ask an AI system how to solve a specific assigned problem. Rephrasing a generated solution using your own words is not acceptable. If you are unsure whether a particular use is allowed, ask me before you submit your work. When you submit an assignment, disclose any collaboration or use of external tools, even when it is permitted."},{"pageId":"assignments/index","pageTitle":"Assignments","heading":"Assignments","anchor":"assignments","text":"Assignments"},{"pageId":"assignments/index","pageTitle":"Assignments","heading":"Online questions","anchor":"online-questions","text":"Online questions Online questions usually follow each lecture and are due at the start of our next class meeting. They are available through Blackboard’s online-question area; deadlines appear in the Blackboard Calendar. Online questions provide a quick chance to practice vocabulary and core skills before we build on the material. Most questions allow multiple attempts."},{"pageId":"assignments/index","pageTitle":"Assignments","heading":"Written homework","anchor":"written-homework","text":"Written homework Assignment Due Homework 1 2026-08-27 23:58 Submit your work through Gradescope using Blackboard’s assignment area. The printable version is available from each assignment page. Weekly homework gives you a chance to work through more involved problems using the techniques we have learned. A complete solution includes both a clear explanation and correct computations. Every exercise is required; point values indicate how much each exercise contributes to the assignment grade. See Example Homework Write-Ups for two sample exercises with sample rubric, full-credit, and partial-credit solutions. Please write your homework by hand. For each exercise, start a new page. Scan your homework to PDF for submission. Submit it on Gradescope and clearly mark the pages associated with every exercise. Solutions will be posted here within one week of the submission deadline. The deadlines will also appear in the Blackboard Calendar."},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Calendar","anchor":"calendar","text":"Calendar I will put all deadlines on the Blackboard Calendar. Please check it regularly."},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Daily lecture log","anchor":"calendar:daily-log","text":"Daily lecture log The public daily log below contains the lectures currently released. Later planning entries remain in the instructor’s private lecture log and do not appear in this navigation. Wk Lec Topics Ref 1 L1: Set notation and basic counting 2026-08-17 Sets, cardinality, Cartesian products, and the sum rule. Set notation and basic counting. L2: Product rule and strings 2026-08-19 Product rule; strings with and without repetition. Set notation and basic counting. L3: More counting techniques 2026-08-21 Subtraction, division, permutations, combinations, and counting models. More counting techniques. 2 L4: Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition 2026-08-24 Lattice paths. Repeated-letter anagrams and multinomial coefficients. Combinations with repetition; stars and bars. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition. L5: Binomial and multinomial expansions; Pascal recursion and restricted lattice paths Tentative 2026-08-26 Choosing the correct model: does order matter and are repetitions allowed. A second bijection for combinations with repetition: increasing integers. Integer solutions with lower bounds. The binomial and multinomial formulas and polynomial expansions. Coefficient extraction from powers of multivariate polynomials. Pascal’s recurrence and triangle: algebraic and combinatorial interpretation. Recursive path counts; paths through or avoiding a specified point; recursive definition; subtraction arguments. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers. L6: More paths; inclusion–exclusion and violation sets Tentative 2026-08-28 Reflection argument and Catalan numbers. Inclusion–exclusion for two, three, and finitely many sets; complement and violation-set formulations. Applications to bounded integer solutions, strings that use every symbol and surjective functions, permutations avoiding specified substrings, and derangements. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers. Inclusion–exclusion and applications. Tentative Lecture topics and assessment dates may change."},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Exams","anchor":"exams","text":"Exams Date Assessment Friday, September 18 Tentative Midterm 1 Friday, October 16 Tentative Midterm 2 Friday, November 13 Tentative Midterm 3 8:00-10:00 a.m., Wednesday, December 9, 2026 Exam 4 and mastery reassessment"},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Key university dates","anchor":"key-university-dates","text":"Key university dates For official University dates, see the Fall 2026 academic calendar and the Registrar’s final-exam schedule. Date Event Monday, August 17 Classes begin Monday, September 7 Labor Day holiday Tuesday, November 24–Friday, November 27 Thanksgiving holiday Wednesday, December 9 Final exam: 8:00-10:00 a.m., Wednesday, December 9, 2026"},{"pageId":"calendar","pageTitle":"Calendar","heading":"Week and unit overview","anchor":"calendar:week-summary","text":"Week and unit overview Weeks Unit Topic 1–4 Tentative Unit I: Enumeration Counting models, bijections, double counting, and inclusion-exclusion 4–5 Tentative Unit II: Generating-function foundations Formal power series and ordinary generating-function operations 6–8 Tentative Unit III: Recurrences State models, linear recurrences, recursive evaluation, and dynamic programming 9–16 Tentative Further topics To be announced"},{"pageId":"index","pageTitle":"Combinatorics","heading":"Combinatorics","anchor":"combinatorics","text":"Combinatorics Course: MATH 31003-01Semester: Fall 2026Instructor: Gennady UraltsevMeetings: Monday, Wednesday, Friday 8:35-9:25 a.m., GEAR 108 Welcome to Combinatorics! We will explore the mathematics of discrete objects: the many ways things can be selected, arranged, connected, and counted. The course covers basic combinatorial techniques, the principle of inclusion-exclusion, generating functions, and recursive relations. Additional topics may include modular arithmetic and applications to encryption, coding theory, graph theory, counting with symmetries, and algebraic structures."},{"pageId":"index","pageTitle":"Combinatorics","heading":"Start here","anchor":"start-here","text":"Start here Syllabus: how the course works, grading, materials, and what to expect. Course calendar: class dates, current topics, holidays, and exams. Assignments: written homework and online questions. Resources: references and student-support links. Lecture notes: notes organized by course unit and lecture. Please check the course calendar for holidays, University deadlines, and topic updates. Course announcements, grades, and submission links are maintained in Blackboard."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/index","pageTitle":"Lecture Notes","heading":"Course units","anchor":"course-units","text":"Course units Unit I: enumeration and inclusion-exclusion. Unit II: generating-function foundations. Unit III: recurrence models. Later units: further topics as announced on the calendar."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/index","pageTitle":"Lecture Notes","heading":"Lecture Notes","anchor":"lecture-notes","text":"Lecture Notes These notes are the main guide through the course. They are organized by unit and mathematical topic rather than by the particular class meeting in which a topic appeared. Draft and future units remain in the instructor’s private course design collection until they are ready for publication."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1. Set notation and basic counting","anchor":"set-notation-and-basic-counting","text":"1. Set notation and basic counting Combinatorics is all about counting objects. We introduce the notation for “containers” of objects a.k.a. sets and define operations on sets. Next, we study the first two counting methods: the sum rule and product rule."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.1 Set notation","anchor":"set-notation","text":"1.1 Set notation A set is a collection of distinct objects. We usually denote a set by a capital letter, such as , and an element of a set by a lowercase letter, such as . The notation means that is an element of , while means that it is not. We can describe a set by listing its elements surrounded by braces. Example 1.1.1 (The decimal-digit set) is the set of decimal digits. Because a set records membership rather than order, . Repeating an element also does not change a set: . We can also describe a set by a property its elements satisfy. If is a specified universe of possible elements, then we use the notation The colon is read as “such that”. Example 1.1.2 (Sets described by a property) is the set of even integers, where denotes the integers. If , then The symbol denotes the empty set, the set with no elements."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.1.1 Set operations","anchor":"set-operations","text":"1.1.1 Set operations Let and be subsets of the same universe. The intersection of and consists of the elements belonging to both sets: The union of and consists of the elements belonging to at least one of the sets: Here “or” is inclusive: an element belonging to both and belongs to their union. The set difference, also called the relative complement, consists of the elements of that do not belong to : The Cartesian product of two sets and is the set of ordered pairs with chosen from and chosen from : For more than two sets we can write . If all the sets are the same we write and so on. Two sets are disjoint if they have no elements in common; equivalently, . Example 1.1.3 (Operations on two finite sets) If and , then"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.1.2 Cardinality and inclusion","anchor":"cardinality-and-inclusion","text":"1.1.2 Cardinality and inclusion For a finite set , its cardinality is the number of elements in . Example 1.1.4 (Cardinalities of finite sets) and . We write if every element of is also an element of : We write if but . Two sets are equal precisely when they have the same elements: Thus, to prove , it is enough to prove both and ."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.1.3 Logical quantifiers and summation notation","anchor":"logical-quantifiers-and-summation-notation","text":"1.1.3 Logical quantifiers and summation notation The symbol means “for every”. The symbol means “there exists”. Example 1.1.5 (Reading quantifiers) means that every element of is nonnegative. says that at least one element of has square . The symbol means “there exists a unique.” Thus asserts two things: at least one element of satisfies , and no two different elements of both satisfy . The notation means that we add the terms as the integer index runs from through : Example 1.1.6 (The sum-of-squares formula in summation notation) An important formula we will prove later in the course is"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.2 Introduction to counting","anchor":"introduction-to-counting","text":"1.2 Introduction to counting"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.2.1 What is a counting problem?","anchor":"counting-problem-model","text":"1.2.1 What is a counting problem? A counting problem asks for the cardinality of a finite set. The first task is therefore to identify that set. We need to know: what one outcome is; when two outcomes are considered different; what restrictions an outcome must satisfy. Example 1.2.1 (Distinguished dice summing to ten) Consider two ordinary six-sided dice, one red and one blue. How many ways can one obtain a roll whose sum is Because the dice are distinguished by color, an outcome is an ordered pair , where is the red result and is the blue result. We can list all of them The outcomes whose sum is are , so there are three. Now let us change the problem in two different ways: Example 1.2.2 (What counts as a reroll outcome?) Variant 1 Consider two ordinary six-sided dice, one red and one blue. If a roll results in both dice having the same number, we reject the roll and try again. How many ways can one obtain a roll whose sum is . Variant 2 Consider two ordinary six-sided dice, one red and one blue. If a roll results in both dice having the same number, we reroll the red die until the two numbers are not the same. How many ways can one obtain a roll whose sum is . It is not fully clear what the problem is asking us. If a “way” records the entire history of rolls, then, in variant 1, rolling and then rerolling and obtaining is different from rolling . Actually, arbitrarily many ties can occur before an accepted result, so there are infinitely many possible histories. Moreover, in variant 2, if one first rolls there is actually no way to ever get as a sum. However, if we count only the final accepted ordered pair, then the problem becomes simple: is not allowed, and the two outcomes with sum are and just as before. This is true whether we reroll both dice (variant 1) after a tie or keep the red die and reroll only the blue die (variant 2). This example shows that it is important to clearly understand what the problem, written in English, means at a precise mathematical level. % horizontal rule in the original Markdown Next, we will develop tools that will help us solve the following challenge problem. You can try to solve it on your own, or come back to it once we covered the necessary counting techniques. For now, you should read and understand it, and use it as a motivating example for the things we will learn next Problem 1.2.3 (Committees with and without a chair) A company has 20 employees and they need to form a committee of 4 people. How many ways can this be done? A company has 20 employees and they need to form a committee of 4 people: 1 chair and 3 other general committee members. How many ways can this be done?"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.2.2 The sum rule","anchor":"the-sum-rule","text":"1.2.2 The sum rule Suppose one object is chosen from either of two finite sets and . If the sets are disjoint, then every object in belongs to exactly one of the two sets. We may therefore add their cardinalities. Theorem 1.2.4 (Sum rule) If and are finite disjoint sets, then Example 1.2.5 (Lowercase letters or decimal digits) A single character may be either a lowercase English letter or a decimal digit. How many allowed characters are there? Solution: Let , and . There are lowercase letters and digits. No character is both a lowercase letter and a digit, so . The sum rule gives If uppercase English letters are also allowed, let be their set. The sets , , and are pairwise disjoint, and the number of allowed characters is The same reasoning extends to any finite number of cases. Theorem 1.2.6 (General sum rule for disjoint sets) Let be finite pairwise disjoint sets. Pairwise disjoint means that Then We can rephrase the sum rule in terms of outcomes of procedures, rather tnan sets. Theorem 1.2.7 (General sum rule for mutually exclusive cases) Suppose the outcomes of a task are divided into mutually exclusive cases , and every outcome belongs to exactly one case. The total number of outcomes is the sum of the numbers in the cases. Problem (Check yourself) In the character example, identify , , and in the general sum rule, and verify pairwise disjointness. Disjointness is important! In the next example, let us see why checking disjointness is very important. Example 1.2.8 (Overlapping brands at two stores) Suppose Amazon sells three brands of juice and Walmart sells five brands. How many different brands can a shopper choose from if they may shop at either store? Wrong solution: The number of brands at amazon is and the number of brands at Walmart is , we need to find the total number of brands . The sum rule tells us that The problem with this solution is that if the stores carry some of the same brands, adding gives duplicates. is the correct answer only if and do not carry brands in common, that is if . Depending on the overlap, the available information permits any total from through . Warning 1.2.9 (Check the sum-rule hypotheses) Before using the sum rule, check that the cases are disjoint and that together they include every outcome being counted."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.2.3 The product rule","anchor":"the-product-rule","text":"1.2.3 The product rule Suppose an outcome is constructed through a fixed sequence of choices. If every complete sequence of choices produces exactly one outcome different choices produce different outcomes, the number of options at each step of the sequence of choices is fixed and does not depend on the choices made in the other steps, then the total number of outcomes is obtained by multiplying the numbers of options at each step. Example 1.2.10 (Two-character lowercase strings) A two-character string is an ordered sequence in which each entry is one of the 26 lowercase letters a-z. Solution: There are choices for and, after any first choice, choices for . Therefore there are such strings. Note: The strings and are different because their entries occur in different positions. The argument allows multiple steps and also allows the specific choices at each step to depend on the other choices made. It is important that the total number of choices at each step remains the same. Theorem 1.2.11 (Product rule) Suppose every outcome is constructed uniquely by a fixed sequence of steps. Assume that, after any valid choices in the earlier steps, step has exactly valid options. Then the number of outcomes is The available options may depend on the earlier choices; what must remain fixed is their number . Example 1.2.12 (Three-letter strings with repetition) How many strings of length 3 made with letters a-z are there? Solution: A string can be specified by first choosing the letter in position 1 (step 1), then the letter in position 2 (step 2), then in position 3 (step 3). For each of the steps there are 26 choices. Every string is obtained from exactly one sequence of such choices (different choices give different strings), so the product rule gives Example 1.2.13 (Three-letter strings without repetition) How many strings of length 3 made with letters a-z are there such that no letter appears more than once? Solution: A string can be specified by first choosing the letter in position 1 (step 1), then the letter in position 2 (step 2) and making sure that it is not the same as the letter chosen in position 1, then choosing a letter in position 3 (step 3) while making sure that it is not the letter chosen in position 1 nor in position 2. Step 1: 26 choices Step 2: 25 choices (26 letters except the one chosen in step 1) Step 3: 24 choices (26 letters except the one chosen in step 1 and the one chosen in step 2) Every valid string is obtained from exactly one sequence of such choices (different choices give different strings), so the product rule gives It is important to note that in this example the specific choices at steps 2 and 3 depend on the choices made in the previous step but the number of possible choices does not! This allows us to apply the product rule. Example 1.2.14 (Three-letter strings with no symbol appearing three times) How many strings of length 3 made with letters a-z are there such that no letter appears more than twice? Wrong/Incomplete solution: A string can be specified by first choosing the letter in position 1 (step 1), then the letter in position 2 (step 2) (without any restrictions, because the second letter can be the same as the first one and not lead to a forbidden outcome) In step 3 we choose the letter for position 3. Step 1: 26 choices Step 2: 26 choices Step 3: ?? choices (it depends, if in step 1 and 2 we chose the same letter then we cannot choose it again so we have 25 choices; if we did not choose the same letter we can choose any letter, so 26 choices. ) Possible correct solution: A string can be specified by choosing by first choosing the letter in position 1 (step 1), then the letter in position 2 (step 2), then the letter in position 3 (step 3), all while making sure not to choose a string with the same letter repeated 3 times. Let us distinguish 2 cases: D: In step 1 and step 2 we choose different letters S: In step 1 and step 2 we choose the same letter By the sum rule, the total amount of strings is going to be since (we cannot both select the same and different letters in step 1 and 2). Let us compute . Since the first two letters are different we have the following options: Step 1: 26 choices Step 2: 25 choices (cannot be the same as step 1) Step 3: 26 choices (since the first are different, there are no restrictions) so by the product rule Let us compute . Since the first two letters are the same we have the following options: Step 1: 26 choices Step 2: 1 choice (must be the same letter as step 1) Step 3: 25 choices (since the first two letters are the same we cannot choose that letter again) so The final answer is ."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.3 Strings: an important type of counting problems","anchor":"strings-model","text":"1.3 Strings: an important type of counting problems The preceding examples illustrate an important type of counting problem: counting ordered sequences of symbols. Definition 1.3.1 (String over an alphabet) Let be a finite set of symbols that we call an alphabet. A string of length over an alphabet is an ordered sequence where each . Using set notation, a string of length is an element of the Cartesian product . The position of each symbol is important. For example, ab and ba are different strings. Two strings are equal precisely when they have the same symbol in every position. Repetition is allowed unless the problem states otherwise. There is also one string of length , called the empty string. It contains no symbols. Theorem 1.3.2 (Strings with repetition) Let be an alphabet with symbols, and let be a nonnegative integer. The number of strings of length over is Proof There are positions. Each position may contain any of the symbols, independently of the symbols chosen in the other positions. The product rule therefore gives When , the product has no factors and is equal to , corresponding to the one empty string. Example 1.3.3 (Three-digit strings with repetition) Let be the alphabet of decimal digits. The number of strings of length over is These are strings rather than three-digit positive integers, so a string such as 042 is included. Suppose now that no symbol may occur more than once. Theorem 1.3.4 (Strings without repetition) Let be an alphabet with symbols, and let be a nonnegative integer. The number of strings of length over with no repeated symbol is This formula makes sense when , the product in the first line has factors. When we have . When then . Proof Assuming that . There are choices for the first position. Once a symbol has been used, it cannot be used again, so there are choices for the second position, choices for the third position, and, finally, choices for the th position. The product rule gives The specific digits available at each step depend on the earlier choices, but the number of available digits does not. This is exactly what the product rule requires. If , a string without repetition would require more than distinct symbols from an alphabet containing only symbols. No such string exists, so . Example 1.3.5 (Three-digit strings without repetition) Let be the alphabet of decimal digits. The number of strings of length with no repeating digits over is These are strings rather than three-digit positive integers, so a string such as 042 is included."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.4 External References","anchor":"external-references","text":"1.4 External References"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/01-set-notation-and-basic-counting","pageTitle":"1. Set notation and basic counting","heading":"1.4.1 Additional material (optional)","anchor":"additional-material-optional","text":"1.4.1 Additional material (optional) Counting Rocks! - Basics of Set Theory Discrete Math II - 6.1.1 The Rules of Sum and Product Counting Rocks! - The addition, subtraction, and multiplication principles"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2. More counting techniques","anchor":"more-counting-techniques","text":"2. More counting techniques We will now add two operations to our counting toolkit. The difference rule counts allowed objects by removing forbidden ones. The division principle corrects a construction that overcounts the same outcome multiple times. We then use these rules to derive formulas for permutations and combinations - important counting problems that differ by whether order is considered important or not. Finally, we discuss how strings and sets can be used to model a variety of different combinatorial problems using bijections."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.1 The difference rule","anchor":"the-difference-rule","text":"2.1 The difference rule We used Theorem 1.2.4 to count the elements in a set by subdividing them into separate cases. When solving problems that impose restrictions on the allowed outcomes, it is convenient to first count the total number of possible outcomes and subtract those that are not allowed. Theorem 2.1.1 (Difference rule) Let be a finite set. If then Equivalently, if is the universe of all possible objects and is the set of forbidden objects, then the number of allowed objects is . Proof Because , the sets and are disjoint and their union is . The sum rule therefore gives Subtracting from both sides proves the formula. Example 2.1.2 (Recounting strings by subtraction) In Example 1.2.14, we counted the length- strings over a-z in which no letter appears three times by separating them into cases. Let us now count the same set using subtraction. Solution: An outcome is an ordered string of three letters. Let be the set of all such strings. Repetition is allowed, so the product rule gives Let be the set of forbidden strings in which one letter appears three times. Such a string is determined by its repeated letter, so . The difference rule gives This is a useful way of counting because forbidden strings are easier to describe than the allowed strings. This agrees with the earlier case-based count, but subtraction produces the shorter model. Warning 2.1.3 (Subtract only objects inside the universe) Suppose and . A careless application of the difference rule gives whereas, actually, The reason for the mistake is that we didn’t check that because . The elements of that can actually be removed are those in . Therefore the correct computation is Before subtracting, always check that the forbidden set is a subset of the universe! If it is not, replace it by its intersection with the universe. Example 2.1.4 (Distinct-digit PINs) A four-digit PIN is a string of four digits from 0-9; leading zeroes are allowed. How many four-digit PINs have no repeated digit and contain at least one odd digit? Incorrect solution: Let be the set of four-digit PINs with no repeated digit. Because a PIN is an ordered string of length 4 chosen from of an alphabet of 10 letters, we get We want to remove the PINs containing no odd digit. The number of strings containing no odd digits is the strings of 4 digits chosen from the alphabet of the 5 odd digits : Therefore we get that This solution is wrong! The issue is that cannot be subtracted from because it is not true that . The way we counted is we included pins containing no odd digits but possibly containing repetitions, like the pin 2248. This pin was not accounted for in so we should not be subtracting it. Correct solution: Instead, let be the set of PINs in whose digits are all even. Then , and its four positions must contain distinct digits chosen from the 5 even digits. Hence The difference rule now gives The modeling lesson is that the universe and the forbidden set must enforce the same background restrictions. Here, “no repeated digit” is part of the definition of both and ."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.1.1 Counting the same set in two ways","anchor":"counting-the-same-set-in-two-ways","text":"2.1.1 Counting the same set in two ways In Example 1.2.14 and Example 2.1.2, we counted the same quantity in two different ways. Our approaches clearly work for any alphabet with letters (it doesn’t have to be the standard 26 letter alphabet) The first approach applied to an alphabet of letters, where gives the formula On the other hand, using the difference rule gives the formula Because both expressions count the same set of strings, we must surely have By itself, this identity is not very exciting. One can show it by standard algebraic simplification. However, it is interesting because it shows how using different combinatorial approaches can provide a “combinatorial” explanation to important formulas from algebra. This idea is often referred to as double counting, and we will see more examples in the future."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.1.2 More examples of subtraction","anchor":"more-examples-of-subtraction","text":"2.1.2 More examples of subtraction Example 2.1.5 (Decimal strings containing a zero) How many length- strings of decimal digits contain at least one 0? Solution: The objects are strings, so order matters, leading zeroes are allowed, and digits may repeat. Let be the set of all length- decimal strings. Then . We could try to count strings according to the position of a zero. However the cases would overlap because there are string that have several zeroes. Instead we exclude the strings with no zero. Each of the six positions has choices, so there are of them. Hence the requested count is The complement replaces overlapping cases with one product-rule count. Example 2.1.6 (A string with prohibition) How many length- strings over the alphabet do not begin with ab? Solution: Let contain all length- strings over the alphabet. Repetition is allowed, so . Let contain the strings beginning with ab. Their first two positions are fixed, and each of the remaining six positions has choices. Thus , and the difference rule gives The restriction fixes two particular positions; it does not forbid a or b in the other positions. Example 2.1.7 (Strings containing at least two different symbols) How many length- strings over an alphabet of letters with , contain at least two different letters? Solution: There are strings in total. If a string does NOT have two different letters then it is just one letter repeated 5 times like aaaaa. So there are forbidden strings. Therefore the quantity we are looking for is Note: for the boundary case (that is an alphabet of one letter), this gives , as it should! There is no way to have at least two different letters if all you have is one letter."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.2 The division principle and permutations","anchor":"the-division-principle-and-permutations","text":"2.2 The division principle and permutations Theorem 1.2.11 requires every outcome to be constructed uniquely by the specified sequence of choices. The division principle handles a controlled failure of that uniqueness. Sometimes a convenient construction is not unique. If every outcome is represented the same number of times, we can correct the overcount. Theorem 2.2.1 (Division rule for constant overcounting) Suppose a construction produces representations, and every unique outcome has exactly representations, where . Then the total number of outcomes is . Proof Let us count all the possible representations . We do it in two steps. First we choose the outcome to which it corresponds. Then, having chosen the outcome, we choose the specific representation that gives that outcome. There are outcomes (choices at step 1) There is representations to choose from once the “outcome” has been established ( choices at step 2). The number of choices at each step does not depend on the choices of the other steps so we apply the product rule to obtain: , and therefore , as desired. Warning 2.2.2 (The overcounting factor must be constant) Division is justified only when every outcome is represented exactly the same number of times. If some outcomes have more representations than others, there is no single overcounting factor by which to divide. Next, we will apply this principle to count strings where the order of characters does not matter, but only the characters actually present do."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.3 Unordered selections: combinations without repetition","anchor":"unordered-selections-combinations-without-repetition","text":"2.3 Unordered selections: combinations without repetition Lecture Note 1 introduced Section 1.3 as ordered objects and Section 1.1 as unordered collections. We now use the division rule to pass from ordered representations to unordered selections. First, we introduce an important mathematical notation, the factorial. Definition 2.3.1 (Factorial) For an integer , we define (pronounced “ factorial”) the quantity that is the product of the numbers from to . We also define , matching the convention that an empty product equals . The factorial is closely related to the concept of order. Indeed let us count the ways to order or arrange a total of distinct characters. Example 2.3.2 (Anagrams: n distinct characters) What is the number of anagrams (permutations) of a string of distinct characters? Solution: A permutation of a string of characters is another length- string over the alphabet consisting of those characters, with no repetition. Therefore the number of anagrams (permutations) of a string of distinct characters is Theorem 2.3.3 (Factorial formula for -permutations) The product defining in Theorem 1.3.4 can be written as The division removes all the numbers from downwards from the product in the numerator: as desired. We now have the notation needed to count unordered selections. A -element subset of a set is a subset with . Definition 2.3.4 (Binomial coefficient) For integers and , the binomial coefficient is the number of -element subsets of an -element set. In other words, it is the number of ways to choose different elements from possible options, disregarding the order in which we choose it. Theorem 2.3.5 (Formula for combinations without repetition) For , Proof Let as associate to each set the list of its elements: if with we write Effectively, we associated to a string of characters chosen from the alphabet . We do not allow repetitions since the same element may not be listed multiple times. The number of strings of length from an alphabet of characters is exactly . However, is it true that every string corresponds to a different set? No! if we take a string or any of its premutations, we will describe the same set. Since the number of permutations of a string of distinct characters is we will be overcounting every outcome (set) times. According to the division rule, the number we are looking for is therefore Example 2.3.6 (Choosing a committee) Return to part 1 of Problem 1.2.3: a company has employees and must form a four-person committee. Solution: An outcome is a four-element subset of the set of employees. The employees are distinct, nobody can be selected twice, and positions on the committee are not distinguished. Therefore the number of committees is If we had selected the four employees in sequence, we would have counted every committee times—once for each ordering of its members. Dividing by turns ordered selections into unordered subsets. Warning 2.3.7 (Do not divide merely because the problem says “choose”) Do not divide by merely because a problem uses the word “choose.” First decide what an outcome is and decide whether the order matters We are now ready to solve the second part of the earlier challenge problem. Example 2.3.8 (Choosing a committee with a chair) Return to part 2 of Problem 1.2.3: a company with employees must select a committee with one chair and three other members. Solution 1: Let us split the selection into two steps. First we select the chair. Then, among the remaining 19 employees, we select a subset of 3 to serve as line members of the commitee. Step 1 gives us 20 choices. In step 2 we select a subset of 3 out of the remaining 19 employees. This can be done in ways. Each of these choices yields a valid committee and each possible commitee is the result of a unique choice in our described procedure. Therefore, by the product rule, the total number we are looking for is Solution 2: Let us select the 4 employees to serve on the commitee in order. This can be done in ways. The first person selected is the chair while the remaining 3 are the line members. Each of these orderred selections yields a valid committee, however different selections can lead to the same outcome. For example abcd and acbd both correspond to a commitee with as chair and as commitee line memebers. By how much do we overcount? Any permutation of the last 3 characters gives the same outcome so every outcome is counted times. By the division principle our answer is then"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.4 Counting problems","anchor":"counting-problems","text":"2.4 Counting problems Many combinatorial problems can be solved by modeling them using settings we have become familiar with: ordered strings or unordered subcollections. In particular, if we manage to esablish a “bijection” (a 1-to-1 and onto relationship) between a problem and another one we know how to solve, then the number of outcomes in both has to be the same. Next we provide a list of examples linking common mathematical counting problems to the ones we already studied."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.4.1 Number of subsets of a set","anchor":"number-of-subsets-of-a-set","text":"2.4.1 Number of subsets of a set Example 2.4.1 (Counting all subsets) Let be a finite set with elements. How many subsets does have? In other words: how large is the collection of all sets that are subsets of ? Solution: First, let us number the elements of from to . To a subset , associate the binary string of length as follows: Every subset produces exactly one binary string. Conversely, every binary string specifies exactly one subset by selecting exactly when . Therefore we have established a bijection between the objects we are counting (subsets) and things we know how to count (strings). There are choices for each of the positions, so there are subsets of . For , the formula gives one subset: the empty set itself."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.4.2 Functions from one set to another","anchor":"functions-from-one-set-to-another","text":"2.4.2 Functions from one set to another For sets and , a function assigns exactly one element to each element . Example 2.4.2 (Functions from one set to another) Suppose and , where . How many functions are there? Solution: List the elements of as . A function is determined uniquely by the string For each input , there are possible outputs, with repetition allowed. The product rule therefore gives functions. When , there is one function from the empty set to , corresponding to the empty string, so the value is . Example 2.4.3 (Injective functions from one set to another) Suppose and , where . How many injective functions are there? Solution: We require that different inputs must receive different outputs. As before, list the elements of as . The function is determined uniquely by the string and the function is injective if and only if the string does not have repeating characters. Therefore we have estabished a bijection between injective functions and strings of length of an alphabet of letters. There are such functions. Careful, it is easy to mess up the role of and in the formulas above! One way to help remeber is to try the case when , that is functions from 1 possible input to possible outputs.."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.5 External References","anchor":"external-references","text":"2.5 External References"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/02-more counting techniques","pageTitle":"2. More counting techniques","heading":"2.5.1 Additional material (optional)","anchor":"additional-material-optional","text":"2.5.1 Additional material (optional) Discrete Math II - 6.1.2 The Complement Rule and Complex Counting Problems Discrete Math II - 6.1.3 The Subtraction and Division Rules Counting Rocks! - The addition, subtraction, and multiplication principles Counting Rocks! - The Division Principles and Overcounting Counting Rocks! - Combinations"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/03-applications-of-combinations-and-repetition","pageTitle":"3. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition","heading":"3. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition","anchor":"applications-of-combinations-and-combinations-with-repetition","text":"3. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition The previous notes introduced strings, permutations, and combinations. The central modeling questions were whether order matters and whether a selection may repeat an object. In these notes we use those questions to count lattice paths and repeated-letter anagrams. We then develop the remaining basic model: unordered selections in which repetition is allowed. After studying these notes, you will be able to do the following. Define a northeast lattice path. Encode a two- or three-dimensional lattice path as a string of steps. Count arrangements with repeated symbols using multinomial coefficients. Recognize an unordered selection with repetition as a multiset. Use stars and bars to count multisets and nonnegative integer solutions. Combine several counting models in a single problem."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/03-applications-of-combinations-and-repetition","pageTitle":"3. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition","heading":"3.1 Choosing the correct model","anchor":"l4:choosing-model","text":"3.1 Choosing the correct model We begin with a selection of objects from available types. Before using a formula, we must decide what one outcome records. Order matters? Repetition allowed? Standard model Number of outcomes Yes Yes Strings Yes No Permutations No No Combinations No Yes Multisets The previous notes established the first three formulas. Stars and bars will establish the last formula later in these notes. In every row, is the number of available types and is the total number selected. For the rows without repetition, we must also have . The words used in a problem do not determine the row. The word ``choose,’’ for example, does not automatically mean that order is irrelevant. Choosing a president and then a vice president creates an ordered pair of people, while choosing two ordinary committee members creates an unordered subset. Warning (The outcome comes first) A formula should not come from keywords alone. We first state what information determines one outcome. Next, we decide whether changing the order or repeating an object changes that outcome."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/03-applications-of-combinations-and-repetition","pageTitle":"3. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition","heading":"3.2 Lattice paths in the plane","anchor":"l4:plane-lattice-paths","text":"3.2 Lattice paths in the plane An integer lattice point in the plane is a point whose coordinates are integers. For nonnegative integers and , a northeast lattice path from to uses only the two unit steps Thus moves one unit to the right and moves one unit up. Every such path must use exactly right steps and exactly up steps. It therefore has steps altogether. Reading the steps from start to finish turns the path into a string containing copies of and copies of . Figure 1 shows one path together with the step string that records it. A path from the origin to the point (5,3) and its step string. The correspondence between paths and step strings goes in both directions. A path determines its string by recording its steps, and a string with copies of and copies of determines a unique path by following those instructions from the origin. This is a bijection. Theorem 3.2.1 (Northeast lattice paths) For nonnegative integers and , the number of northeast lattice paths from to is Proof Each path determines a unique length- string containing copies of and copies of . We choose the positions occupied by the right steps. The remaining positions must contain up steps, so there are paths. Equivalently, we may choose the positions occupied by the up steps, giving . Example 3.2.2 (Paths from to ) A path from to must contain five right steps and three up steps. It has eight steps in total, so the number of paths is The path in Figure 1 corresponds to the string RURRURRU. Its five copies of R and three copies of U verify that it has the required endpoint. The same idea counts paths between two other lattice points. To move from to using only right and up steps, we need right steps and up steps. Such a path exists only when and . Problem (Practice) How many northeast lattice paths go from to ? What are the number of right steps, the number of up steps, and the total number of steps? A rectangular grid with a blue path from (0,0) to (5,3). The path has step string RURRURRU."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/03-applications-of-combinations-and-repetition","pageTitle":"3. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition","heading":"3.3 Three-dimensional paths and multinomial coefficients","anchor":"l4:multinomial-paths","text":"3.3 Three-dimensional paths and multinomial coefficients The string model extends naturally to higher dimensions. In three dimensions, the positive coordinate steps are We begin with a concrete path and build its step string one kind of step at a time. Example 3.3.1 (Filling the positions of a three-dimensional path) How many positive-coordinate lattice paths go from to ? Solution: Throughout this example, we display X steps in blue, Y steps in orange, and Z steps in purple. Every path has six steps: two X steps, three Y steps, and one Z step. Before making any choices, picture six empty positions: This is a multistep operation. In the first stage, we choose which two of the six positions will contain X. There are choices. For example, one possible first-stage choice is . This is only a partial string, not yet a complete path. In the second stage, we choose three of the four positions that are still empty and put Y in them. Thus this stage has choices. Continuing the particular first-stage choice displayed above, one possibility is . One position remains, so it must contain Z: . If we record this forced final stage as a choice, it has possible outcome. Every complete path arises from exactly one choice at each stage, so the product rule gives For a general three-dimensional endpoint , there are positions. The same three stages contribute The final factor always equals , because . This is exactly what we should expect. The last stage is not a new choice among several possibilities: after we choose the and positions, we fill every remaining position with . The same position-filling procedure works for any number of symbol types. A length- string may need to contain copies of symbol , copies of symbol , and so on through copies of symbol , where We start with empty positions. First, we choose the positions for symbol . Next, we choose the positions for symbol , but only from the positions still empty. After those two stages, positions remain. We continue in this way. At the final stage, we choose the positions for symbol from the positions still empty. This final binomial coefficient equals , as it should: the sum condition says that exactly positions remain, and we fill all of them with the last symbol. Definition 3.3.2 (Multinomial coefficient) For and nonnegative integers whose sum is , the multinomial coefficient is the number of strings of length containing exactly copies of symbol for each . It is also commonly written as . Theorem 3.3.3 (Formula for a multinomial coefficient) For and nonnegative integers satisfying , Proof The stages described above have choices, respectively. The choice at one stage determines which positions are available at the next, but the number of choices at the next stage is always the displayed binomial coefficient. Therefore the product rule gives The crossed-out in the denominator of the first factor cancels with the same factorial in the numerator of the second. Next, the two copies of cancel. The same cancellation continues from one factor to the next through every intermediate factorial. The only remaining factorial of this form is in the denominator, and . To apply this formula to paths, we write for the number of steps in coordinate direction . Thus, in dimensions, a positive-coordinate path from the origin to has steps, and the number of such paths is"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/03-applications-of-combinations-and-repetition","pageTitle":"3. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition","heading":"3.4 Anagrams with repeated letters","anchor":"l4:anagrams","text":"3.4 Anagrams with repeated letters An anagram of a word is a rearrangement of all of its letters. If all letters are distinct, there are anagrams of a word of length . Repeated letters make some of those arrangements indistinguishable. Theorem 3.4.1 (Anagrams of a word with repeated letters) If a nonempty word of length has distinct kinds of letters and letter type occurs times, where then the number of anagrams is Proof An anagram is exactly a string with a prescribed number of copies of each letter, so the result follows from Theorem 3.3.3. Here is a second explanation of the denominator. Temporarily distinguish equal letters with labels. For instance, we write the three copies of as . The labeled letters have arrangements. After we erase the labels, each unlabeled anagram appears times, because we can permute the labels within every repeated letter type independently. The division principle gives the stated quotient. Example 3.4.2 (Anagrams of BANANA) The word BANANA has six letters: three copies of A, two copies of N, and one copy of B. Therefore it has distinct anagrams. We can also choose the three positions for A, then two of the three remaining positions for N; the last position contains B. This gives Warning 3.4.3 (Divide only for genuinely interchangeable copies) If two people, colors, or roles are distinct in the outcome, exchanging them does not create the same outcome. A factorial belongs in the denominator only when permuting the corresponding labeled copies leaves the underlying outcome unchanged."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/03-applications-of-combinations-and-repetition","pageTitle":"3. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition","heading":"3.5 Combinations with repetition","anchor":"l4:combinations-with-repetition","text":"3.5 Combinations with repetition A doughnut shop offers ten types of doughnuts and has an unlimited supply of each type. How many ways are there to buy six doughnuts? An outcome is not a string of six successive purchases. For example, buying a glazed doughnut and then a chocolate doughnut gives the same purchase as buying the chocolate doughnut first and the glazed doughnut second. Order does not matter. On the other hand, the buyer may purchase several doughnuts of one type, so the model allows repetition. This is an unordered selection with repetition, also called a multiset. One way to record the purchase is by a multiplicity vector where is the number of doughnuts of type . The entries must satisfy Thus the doughnut problem is equivalent to counting nonnegative integer solutions of an equation."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/03-applications-of-combinations-and-repetition","pageTitle":"3. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition","heading":"3.5.1 The stars-and-bars encoding","anchor":"l4:stars-and-bars-encoding","text":"3.5.1 The stars-and-bars encoding We encode a nonnegative solution of using stars and bars. The stars are the selected objects, and the bars separate the types. Reading from left to right, is the number of stars before the first bar; is the number of stars between bars and ; is the number of stars after the last bar. For example, when and , we encode the vector by Figure 2 displays the same encoding with the four groups of stars separated visually. The stars-and-bars encoding of the vector (2,1,2,2). Bars may be adjacent, because we may select a type zero times. A bar may also occur first or last, corresponding respectively to or . These are valid encodings, not exceptional cases. Theorem 3.5.1 (Stars and bars) For integers and , the number of nonnegative integer solutions to is Proof Every solution determines one string containing stars and bars, and every such string determines one solution by counting the stars in its groups. These two operations undo one another, so this is a bijection. The string has positions. We choose the positions occupied by bars; all remaining positions contain stars. This gives strings. Equivalently, choosing the star positions gives . Corollary 3.5.2 (Combinations with repetition) If types are available and we select objects, with order irrelevant and repetition allowed, then the number of selections is Example 3.5.3 (Buying six doughnuts) Let us return to the shop with ten types of doughnuts. The multiplicities satisfy Stars and bars uses six stars and nine bars, so the number of purchases is The value would count ordered strings of six choices and would count the same purchase many times. The value would forbid buying two doughnuts of the same type. Neither model describes the stated outcome. Remark 3.5.4 (If every variable must be positive) When for every , we first give one object to each type and write . Then and When , the number of positive integer solutions is therefore If , there are no positive solutions. Problem (Practice) How many nonnegative integer solutions are there to Which stars-and-bars string encodes the particular solution ? Seven blue stars are split by three vertical bars into groups of two, one, two, and two."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/03-applications-of-combinations-and-repetition","pageTitle":"3. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition","heading":"3.6 Combining models: nonadjacent letters in ARKANSAS","anchor":"l4:arkansas","text":"3.6 Combining models: nonadjacent letters in ARKANSAS We finish with a problem that uses repeated-letter anagrams, the product rule, and an ordinary combination. Example 3.6.1 (ARKANSAS with no adjacent copies of A) How many anagrams of ARKANSAS have no two copies of A adjacent? Solution: The word contains three copies of A, two copies of S, and one copy each of R, K, and N. We first remove the three copies of A. Next, we arrange the five remaining letters RKNSS. Because the two copies of S are identical, the number of base arrangements is Every base arrangement has six gaps: before the first letter, between each pair of consecutive letters, and after the last letter. To prevent adjacent copies of A, we place at most one A in each gap. We must choose three distinct gaps from the six available gaps, which can be done in ways. By the product rule, the requested number of anagrams is This construction counts each valid anagram exactly once. Given a valid anagram, deleting its three copies of A recovers one unique base arrangement of RKNSS; the former locations of the copies of A recover the three chosen gaps. Figure 3 illustrates the gap construction for one base arrangement. Choosing three gaps around one arrangement of RKNSS. There is a useful consistency check. If we allowed adjacent copies of A, stars and bars would distribute the three indistinguishable copies among the six gaps with repetition. This gives distributions. Thus the total number of unrestricted anagrams would be Direct use of the multinomial formula gives which agrees. When we forbid adjacency, the gap selection changes from a combination with repetition to an ordinary combination without repetition. The base arrangement RSKNS has six gaps. Copies of A occupy the first, third, and sixth gaps, producing ARSAKNSA."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/03-applications-of-combinations-and-repetition","pageTitle":"3. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition","heading":"3.7 A counting checklist","anchor":"l4:checklist","text":"3.7 A counting checklist The main ideas in these notes are different views of the same objects. A lattice path is a step string; an anagram is a string with prescribed symbol multiplicities; a multiset is a multiplicity vector; and a multiplicity vector is a stars-and-bars string. When solving a new problem, we can organize the model with the following checklist. What information determines one outcome? Does order matter, and does the model allow repetition? Which familiar encoding fits: a string, subset, multiset, integer vector, or lattice path? Why is the encoding reversible, or why is any overcounting factor constant? Which formula corresponds to that model? The formulas developed here are"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/03-applications-of-combinations-and-repetition","pageTitle":"3. Applications of combinations and combinations with repetition","heading":"3.8 Further reading","anchor":"further-reading","text":"3.8 Further reading The course resources page links to the books used as references for these notes. Applied Combinatorics, Sections 2.5 and 2.7, covers lattice paths and multinomial coefficients. Counting Rocks!, Sections 3.5.1, 4.1, 4.5, and 4.6, covers multisets, stars and bars, lattice paths, and repeated-letter anagrams."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","anchor":"binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","text":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers The preceding notes developed combinations, multinomial coefficients, lattice paths, and stars and bars as counting models. A new problem rarely announces which model it needs, so we begin by comparing four similar-looking choices whose outcomes are very different. Then, we will see how the coefficients appear in many surprising circumstances: in expansions of polynomials, in Pascal’s triangle, and recursive lattice-path counts. Finally, imposing restrictions on those paths leads to hard counting problem. A famous one is that of the Catalan numbers and the reflection principle that counts them. These numbers also appear in counting ways to parenthesise expressions. After studying these notes, you will be able to do the following. Decide which kind of choice describes a problem by determining whether order matters and whether repetition is allowed. Model combinations with repetition as multiplicity vectors and count them. Explain the relationship among stars and bars, cumulative totals, weakly increasing sequences, and strictly increasing sequences. Translate lower bounds for integer solutions so that stars and bars applies. Use the binomial and multinomial theorems to extract coefficients from powers of polynomials. Verify Pascal’s recurrence algebraically and prove it combinatorially. Compute lattice-path counts recursively and count paths that pass through or avoid a specified point. Use tail reflection to derive the Catalan-number formula and relate Catalan paths to balanced-parenthesis strings."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.1 A bakery problem: four kinds of choice","anchor":"l5:bakery-problem","text":"4.1 A bakery problem: four kinds of choice A bakery sells ten types of doughnuts. A manager wants to buy six doughnuts for six employees. Four different purchasing scenarios will help us compare the available models. Problem 4.1.1 (Buying doughnuts for six employees) Which standard kind of choice describes each scenario? The manager decides which type of doughnut to buy for each employee and may choose the same type for several employees. The manager again chooses one type for each employee, but decides not to buy the same type twice. The manager selects six different types and lets the employees decide who receives which one. The manager buys six doughnuts to bring to the employees. Several doughnuts may have the same type, and the manager does not assign them to particular employees."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.1.1 Modeling the four scenarios","anchor":"modeling-the-four-scenarios","text":"4.1.1 Modeling the four scenarios The important question is not only whether the manager purchases six doughnuts. We must ask what information one outcome retains. In the first scenario, the employees play the role of six labeled positions. For each employee, the manager chooses one of ten types and may repeat the same type. An outcome is a length- string of doughnut types, or equivalently a function from the employees to the types. The number of choices is \\[ 10^6. \\] The employees are still labeled, so assigning glazed to Alice and chocolate to Bob is different from assigning chocolate to Alice and glazed to Bob. Repetition is now forbidden. This is a -permutation of the ten types, and the number of choices is \\[ P(10,6)=\\frac{10!}{4!}. \\] The manager selects the six types before any employee receives one. Order and employee assignments are not part of the purchase, and the manager repeats no type. This is an ordinary combination, with \\[ C(10,6) \\] possible selections. Order and employee assignments are again irrelevant, but repetition is allowed. The manager might buy six glazed doughnuts, six different types, or anything in between. This is a combination with repetition, and the number of choices is \\[ C(10+6-1,6)=C(15,6). \\] The four scenarios occupy the four rows of the familiar choice table: In this table, is the number of available types and is the number of objects selected. The bakery story shows why the wording of the problem is not enough by itself: the mathematical model depends on what counts as a different outcome."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.2 Combinations with repetition and cumulative totals","anchor":"l5:combinations-with-repetition","text":"4.2 Combinations with repetition and cumulative totals Let us return to the fourth bakery scenario. If is the number of doughnuts of type , then a nonnegative integer solution describes a purchase: More generally, selecting objects from types with repetition is the same problem as finding the nonnegative integer solutions of"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.2.1 The separator count","anchor":"the-separator-count","text":"4.2.1 The separator count The stars-and-bars construction places stars in a row and uses separators to divide them among the types. There are positions altogether. Choosing the separator positions gives solutions. Choosing the star positions instead gives the equal expression Thus the six-doughnut purchase in the fourth scenario can be made in ways. Can we explain this count using another combinatorial encoding? Yes. We can record the separator positions as cumulative totals."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.2.2 Cumulative totals and weakly increasing sequences","anchor":"l5:cumulative-totals","text":"4.2.2 Cumulative totals and weakly increasing sequences A sequence is weakly increasing if each term is at least the preceding term: It is strictly increasing if each inequality is strict: Repeated values are allowed in a weakly increasing sequence but not in a strictly increasing sequence. For a nonnegative solution of , we define Since every is nonnegative, The number records how many stars appear before separator . Two separators may have the same number of stars before them, which is why the sequence is only weakly increasing. The final total is forced, so we omit it from the encoding. This construction is reversible. If numbers satisfy , then we recover Thus solutions of are in bijection with weakly increasing sequences of length selected from Such a sequence is a choice with repetition of elements from possible values. Applying the combination-with-repetition formula with these roles exchanged gives exactly as in the separator count. Example 4.2.1 (Encoding the separators by cumulative totals) The solution of gives In a stars-and-bars string, the three separators occur after , , and stars. Conversely, the differences recover ."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.2.3 An alternative proof of the combinations with repetitions formula","anchor":"l5:strictly-increasing-proof","text":"4.2.3 An alternative proof of the combinations with repetitions formula We already know that the number of nonnegative integer solutions of is . We obtained this answer by arranging stars and separators. Let us now obtain the same answer in a different way, using an ordinary combination. An ordinary combination is a choice of distinct elements from a set. Thus our plan is to encode each solution as a subset. The cumulative totals from the preceding section almost do this, but they are only weakly increasing: two consecutive totals can be equal when some is zero. To make the totals strictly increasing, we first make every part positive. Theorem 4.2.2 (Combinations with repetition, revisited) The number of nonnegative integer solutions of is Equivalently, this is the number of ways to select objects from types when repetition is allowed. Proof We start with a nonnegative solution and define We have added one to each of the variables, so More importantly, every is positive. This guarantees that the partial sums increase at every step. Next, we record the first partial sums: Since each is at least , these numbers satisfy The final inequality holds because at least must remain for . We have therefore obtained an ordinary -element subset We must check that this encoding does not lose any information. For any -element subset of , we write its elements in increasing order: We recover the positive integers by taking consecutive differences: Every one of these numbers is positive. Moreover, their sum telescopes: Finally, we define . The resulting are nonnegative, and their sum is , so they form a solution of the original equation. The two constructions undo one another. We have therefore established a bijection between the solutions of and the -element subsets of . The number of such subsets is Example 4.2.3 (Following one solution through the bijection) Let us follow the earlier solution of . Adding to each entry gives whose sum is . The first three partial sums are Thus the solution corresponds to the three-element subset of . Starting with , consecutive differences recover which gives . Subtracting from each entry returns the original solution . Remark 4.2.4 (From weakly increasing to strictly increasing) There is a direct connection between this proof and the weakly increasing sequence from the preceding section. We had Since , the new partial sums satisfy Thus we turn a weakly increasing sequence into a strictly increasing one by adding to its first term, to its second term, and so on: Subtracting from the th term reverses the transformation. Therefore is equivalent to"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.2.4 Solutions with lower bounds","anchor":"l5:lower-bounds","text":"4.2.4 Solutions with lower bounds Stars and bars applies directly when every variable may be any nonnegative integer. If a variable must instead satisfy , reserve its required amount first. The part still available for distribution is then counted with nonnegative variables. Theorem 4.2.5 (Integer solutions with lower bounds) For integers , the number of integer solutions to is when . If that difference is negative, there are no solutions. Proof We set . Then , and the original equation becomes The substitution is reversible: from a solution in the , recover . Stars and bars applied to the transformed equation gives the formula. Example 4.2.6 (Asymmetric lower bounds) How many integer solutions satisfy Solution: We define The new variables are nonnegative and satisfy Therefore the number of solutions is Warning (Lower bounds and upper bounds behave differently) The method we saw allows us to map an integer solution problem with a lower bound (for example ) to a problem of counting non-negative integer solutions. This method does not work if we have upper bound conditions, for example . We can still solve the problem when there is an upper bound condition on only one variable, by using the subtraction principle. However, with several upper bounds, such as and will require us to use the inclusion–exclusion principle."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.2.5 Summary","anchor":"summary","text":"4.2.5 Summary The number of nonnegative integer solutions of \\[ x_1+x_2+\\cdots+x_n=k \\] is \\[ C(k+n-1,n-1)=C(k+n-1,k). \\] If the variables have lower bounds , we subtract the lower bounds first. The remaining total is . Cumulative totals encode a solution as a weakly increasing sequence. Adding to its th term converts it into a strictly increasing sequence, which is the same as an ordinary subset."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.3 The binomial theorem","anchor":"l5:binomial-theorem","text":"4.3 The binomial theorem Let us consider the product We obtain every raw term in its expansion by choosing either or from each factor. To obtain , exactly of the factors must contribute ; all other factors contribute . The coefficient is therefore the number of ways to choose those factors. Theorem 4.3.1 (Binomial theorem) For every nonnegative integer , Proof Label the factors by their positions. The subset of positions from which we choose determines a term. If that subset has size , the resulting term is . There are such subsets. Grouping the raw terms by gives the displayed expansion. For example, row of the binomial coefficients gives The same reasoning works when the two terms have numerical weights. Example 4.3.2 (Extracting a weighted coefficient) What is the coefficient of in ? Solution: To obtain , we choose from exactly four of the seven factors and choose from the remaining three. The coefficient is The binomial coefficient chooses the contributing factors; the powers and record the numerical contribution from those choices. Example 4.3.3 (Accounting for an outside power) What is the coefficient of in ? Solution: The outside factor already contributes , so we need from . We choose from six factors. The coefficient is Two useful identities follow immediately from the binomial theorem. Setting gives which also counts all subsets of an -element set by their sizes. For , setting and gives Equivalently, the sum of the even-indexed entries in row equals the sum of the odd-indexed entries."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.4 The multinomial theorem","anchor":"l5:multinomial-theorem","text":"4.4 The multinomial theorem When every factor offers choices rather than two, a multinomial coefficient counts the multiplicities of the choices. Theorem 4.4.1 (Multinomial theorem) For every integer and every nonnegative integer , where the sum ranges over all nonnegative integer solutions of . Proof We choose one term from each of the labeled factors. A raw term equals exactly when the resulting choice string contains copies of for every . The number of such strings is Grouping equal raw terms proves the formula. Example 4.4.2 (A multinomial coefficient with weights) What is the coefficient of in ? Solution: The exponents sum to , so the requested term can occur. We choose the two factors contributing , the three contributing , and the one contributing . The coefficient is Before we compute a multinomial coefficient, we check the total degree. For example, the coefficient of in is zero because the requested exponents sum to , while every term in the expansion has total degree . Remark 4.4.3 (How many distinct monomials appear?) A nonnegative solution of determines a monomial in . Stars and bars shows that the number of distinct monomials is ."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.5 Pascal’s recurrence and Pascal’s triangle","anchor":"l5:pascal","text":"4.5 Pascal’s recurrence and Pascal’s triangle The factorial formula is not the only way to compute binomial coefficients. A local counting argument relates each interior entry to two entries in the preceding row. Theorem 4.5.1 (Pascal’s recurrence) If and , then The boundary values are . Proof (Algebraic verification) Using the factorial formula and a common denominator, Proof (Combinatorial proof) For an -element set with a distinguished element , we partition the -element subsets of into two classes. If a subset does not contain , we choose all elements from the remaining elements, giving possibilities. If it contains , we choose its other elements from the remaining , giving possibilities. The two classes are disjoint and exhaustive, so the sum rule gives the recurrence. Starting with the boundary entries and using the recurrence produces Pascal’s triangle: We obtain entry by adding the two entries immediately above it. The symmetry is visible in the triangle and corresponds to choosing the included elements or, equivalently, the omitted elements."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.6 Recursive lattice-path counts","anchor":"l5:path-recursion","text":"4.6 Recursive lattice-path counts For nonnegative integers and , we write for the number of northeast lattice paths from to . If , a path reaches either from by a right step or from by an up step. These cases are disjoint, so Along the axes there is only one path, so . These are the same recurrence and boundary data as Pascal’s triangle. Therefore in agreement with the step-string argument from the preceding notes. We can use the recurrence to fill an entire grid. We begin with along the coordinate axes. At every interior vertex, we add the labels at the vertex to its left and the vertex below it. Figure 1 shows this computation near the point . A recursive table of northeast-path counts. The highlighted point $(a,b)=(4,3)$ can be reached from $(3,3)$ in $20$ ways and from $(4,2)$ in $15$ ways, so its label is $20+15=35$. This table is a rotated portion of Pascal’s triangle. It also suggests a way to handle forbidden vertices when no direct formula is available: we assign a forbidden vertex the value zero, then continue filling the table by the same left-plus-below rule. A seven-column by six-row lattice whose vertices are labeled by path counts. The vertex (4,3), labeled 35, is highlighted in red. Its left neighbor (3,3), labeled 20, and lower neighbor (4,2), labeled 15, are highlighted in orange, with arrows pointing to (4,3)."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.6.1 Paths through a specified point","anchor":"paths-through-a-specified-point","text":"4.6.1 Paths through a specified point For and , a path from to that passes through decomposes uniquely into a path from to followed by a path from to . The product rule gives The total number of paths to is , so the number avoiding is Example 4.6.1 (Passing through or avoiding ) A path from to that passes through consists of a path with four right and seven up steps, followed by a path with six right and three up steps. Thus the number passing through the point is There are paths altogether, so the number avoiding is Warning 4.6.2 (Feasibility of the specified point) The product formula assumes that is coordinatewise between the start and endpoint. A northeast path from to cannot pass through a point with a negative coordinate, an -coordinate greater than , or a -coordinate greater than ."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.7 Catalan paths and the reflection principle","anchor":"l5:catalan-paths","text":"4.7 Catalan paths and the reflection principle There are northeast paths from to . We now impose a global restriction: at every point of the path, require In other words, the path may touch the diagonal , but it may never go above it. A path satisfying this restriction is called a Catalan path or a Dyck path. Definition 4.7.1 (Catalan numbers) The th Catalan number is the number of northeast lattice paths from to that never go above the line . We include the empty path, so . For , Figure 2 displays all five paths that obey the diagonal restriction. The five Catalan paths for $n=3$. The corresponding step strings and balanced-parenthesis strings are displayed below the paths. To count the valid paths, we subtract the paths that cross the diagonal. The reflection principle gives a bijective count of those bad paths. Figure 3 previews the transformation: reflection leaves the path unchanged until its first crossing and reflects the remaining tail. Reflecting the tail after the first crossing changes a bad path to $(4,4)$ into a path to $(3,5)$. The construction is reversible. Theorem 4.7.2 (Catalan formula) For every nonnegative integer , Proof For , the one path is empty, and both displayed expressions equal . Now assume . A bad path has a first point at which it reaches the line . We keep the path unchanged up to that point and reflect the tail—the portion from the first crossing to the endpoint—across the line . The crossing point lies on the reflecting line and therefore stays fixed. On the tail, reflection exchanges right steps and up steps. Reflection across sends a point to . In particular, it sends the original endpoint to . The resulting path therefore goes from to . This map is reversible. Any northeast path from to must reach the line . We keep its prefix through the first point on that line and reflect its tail across the line. This produces a bad path to , and reflecting the same tail again recovers the original path. Thus bad paths to are in bijection with all paths to . There are such paths. Subtracting them from all paths gives the first formula. Finally, so the difference is . The first Catalan numbers are For , the five Catalan step strings are Five separate three-by-three grids show all northeast paths from (0,0) to (3,3) that stay on or below the diagonal. The paths are labeled RRRUUU, RRURUU, RRUURU, RURRUU, and RURURU, together with their balanced-parenthesis strings. Two grids compare a bad path from (0,0) to (4,4) with a path from (0,0) to (3,5). Both paths have the same blue prefix ending at the first point (2,3) on the line y equals x plus 1. The orange tail of the first path is reflected across that line to produce the orange tail of the second path."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.7.1 Balanced-parenthesis strings","anchor":"balanced-parenthesis-strings","text":"4.7.1 Balanced-parenthesis strings We replace every right step by a left parenthesis and every up step by a right parenthesis. A path ends at exactly when the string contains parentheses of each kind. The condition says that every initial segment contains at least as many left parentheses as right parentheses. This is exactly the condition for a balanced-parenthesis string. Hence also counts balanced strings of pairs of parentheses. For example, the five paths above correspond to Conversely, replacing each left parenthesis by and each right parenthesis by recovers a unique Catalan path. The two replacements undo one another, so this correspondence is a bijection. This is an important combinatorial lesson: a named sequence is usually useful because it counts many families of objects, not only because it has a closed formula. Remark 4.7.3 (A recurrence to revisit later) A first-return decomposition of a nonempty Catalan path gives A later note on combinatorial recurrences will develop this nonlinear recurrence. For now, the reflection argument supplies the closed formula. Problem (Practice) How many northeast paths from to never go above ? How many go above the diagonal at least once? Which count comes from subtraction, and which comes from the reflection bijection?"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.8 Summary of the counting connections","anchor":"l5:summary","text":"4.8 Summary of the counting connections The main formulas in these notes arise from a small number of reversible encodings and counting rules: In each case, the formula becomes easier to remember after identifying the objects behind it: shifted sequences, translated integer vectors, choices from labeled factors, subsets split by a distinguished element, concatenated paths, or reflected paths."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/04-binomial-expansions-pascal-paths-and-catalan-numbers","pageTitle":"4. Binomial expansions, Pascal paths, and Catalan numbers","heading":"4.9 Further reading","anchor":"further-reading","text":"4.9 Further reading The course resources page links to the books used as references for these notes. Applied Combinatorics, Sections 2.5–2.7, covers lattice paths and binomial and multinomial expansions. Counting Rocks!, Sections 4.1–4.3 and 4.5–4.6, covers Pascal’s recurrence, binomial identities, and multinomial counting. Discrete Mathematics: An Open Introduction, Sections 3.1–3.2, presents coefficient and lattice-path interpretations. Combinatorics Through Guided Discovery, Section 1.3.1, also discusses Catalan paths and the reflection principle."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/05-inclusion-exclusion-and-applications","pageTitle":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications","heading":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications","anchor":"inclusion-exclusion-and-applications","text":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications The sum rule applies directly to disjoint sets. When sets overlap, ordinary addition counts some objects more than once. The principle of inclusion–exclusion repairs this overcount systematically. More importantly, it gives a general strategy for restrictions: we begin with a convenient universe, describe each way an outcome can violate the restrictions, and count intersections of those violation sets. After studying these notes, you will be able to do the following. Explain the correction terms in the two-set and three-set formulas. State and justify the finite inclusion–exclusion formula. Express a desired set as the complement of a union of violation sets. Count bounded integer solutions by combining inclusion–exclusion with stars and bars. Count strings and surjective functions that use every available symbol. Apply block counting and inclusion–exclusion to forbidden substrings. Derive the formula for derangements and count permutations with a specified number of fixed points."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/05-inclusion-exclusion-and-applications","pageTitle":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications","heading":"5.1 Correcting for overlap","anchor":"l6:correcting-overlap","text":"5.1 Correcting for overlap If finite sets and overlap, the sum counts every element of twice: once as an element of and once as an element of . We subtract one copy of the intersection to count the union. Theorem 5.1.1 (Inclusion–exclusion for two sets) For finite sets and , Example 5.1.2 (Students in two courses) In a group of students, take algebra, take geometry, and take both. Then the number taking at least one of the two courses is Each of the first two counts includes the eight students in both courses, so subtracting eight leaves one copy of each. For three sets, subtracting all pairwise intersections removes too much from the elements common to all three sets. Such an element is first counted three times and then subtracted three times, leaving a net contribution of zero. We must add the triple intersection once. Theorem 5.1.3 (Inclusion–exclusion for three sets) For finite sets , , and , Example 5.1.4 (Students in three courses) There are mathematics majors. Of these, take algebra, take geometry, and take calculus. The three pairwise intersection sizes are , , and , and five students take all three courses. The number taking at least one of the courses is Consequently, take none of the three courses."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/05-inclusion-exclusion-and-applications","pageTitle":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications","heading":"5.2 The finite inclusion–exclusion principle","anchor":"l6:finite-pie","text":"5.2 The finite inclusion–exclusion principle For a nonnegative integer , we write , with . If is nonempty, the notation means that all conditions indexed by hold simultaneously. Theorem 5.2.1 (Principle of inclusion–exclusion) For an integer and finite sets , In words, we add the sizes of the individual sets, subtract the sizes of all pairwise intersections, add the sizes of all triple intersections, and then we continue with alternating signs. Proof For an element that belongs to exactly of the sets, the formula counts once for every way to choose one of those sets, subtracts it once for every way to choose two, and so on. Its total contribution is The binomial theorem applied to gives Since , the first displayed alternating sum equals . Thus the formula counts every element in the union exactly once and counts no element outside the union. Sometimes it is convenient to group intersections by their number of sets. We define Then inclusion–exclusion becomes When all -fold intersections have the same size, is that common size multiplied by ."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/05-inclusion-exclusion-and-applications","pageTitle":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications","heading":"5.2.1 The complement or violation-set form","anchor":"the-complement-or-violation-set-form","text":"5.2.1 The complement or violation-set form Most applications ask for outcomes satisfying none of several bad conditions. We write for the universe of all outcomes under consideration and for the set of outcomes with violation . The valid outcomes form Taking the complement of the union formula gives where we interpret the term for as . For a restricted counting problem, we use the following workflow. First, we choose a convenient universe containing every valid outcome and whose size we can count. Next, we define one violation set for each forbidden condition. We state explicitly that the desired set is . For a specified index set , we count by imposing all violations in at once. Finally, we sum with alternating signs and group intersections of equal size when possible. Warning 5.2.2 (The intersections are the real work) Inclusion–exclusion is not a substitute for modeling. The formula becomes useful only after we define the universe and violation sets and learn how we can count their intersections. The formula does not require pairwise disjointness; the alternating terms correct exactly that overlap."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/05-inclusion-exclusion-and-applications","pageTitle":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications","heading":"5.3 Application: bounded integer solutions","anchor":"l6:bounded-solutions","text":"5.3 Application: bounded integer solutions Translating variables removes lower bounds. Upper bounds are harder because a solution may violate several of them simultaneously. This makes bounded integer solutions a natural application of inclusion–exclusion. Example 5.3.1 (Four bounded variables) How many integer solutions satisfy Solution: We take to be the set of all nonnegative solutions and temporarily ignore the upper bounds. Stars and bars gives For , we define The desired solutions are those in none of the four violation sets. If specified variables violate their upper bounds, we subtract from each of them. The transformed nonnegative variables sum to , so a specified -fold intersection has solutions whenever . There are choices for the violating coordinates. Hence the requested count is The intersection of all four violation sets is empty because four variables cannot each be at least while their sum is . There is also a useful check. Replacing by gives a bijection with nonnegative solutions whose sum is . The upper bounds are automatic for a sum of , so stars and bars gives again. The same argument gives a general formula. Theorem 5.3.2 (A common upper bound) For an integer and nonnegative integers and , The number of integer solutions to is because larger values of would force the transformed total to be negative. Proof We take to be the set of all nonnegative solutions, with the upper bounds temporarily removed. For each , we take to be the solutions in which . The desired solutions form . For a set of indices, in the intersection , we subtract from each variable indexed by . The transformed variables are nonnegative and have total , so stars and bars gives solutions when that total is nonnegative. There are choices for . The complement form of inclusion–exclusion gives the stated sum, and the upper limit includes exactly the feasible values of . If a problem has lower and upper bounds, we first translate the lower bounds. At the same time, we translate the upper bounds and then apply inclusion–exclusion to the new variables. Problem (Practice) What inclusion–exclusion expression, before simplification, counts the nonnegative solutions of What are the universe and the three violation sets in this model?"},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/05-inclusion-exclusion-and-applications","pageTitle":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications","heading":"5.4 Application: strings using every symbol","anchor":"l6:all-symbols","text":"5.4 Application: strings using every symbol A positive requirement such as ``every symbol appears’’ is often easier to enforce by excluding the negative events ``symbol is missing.’’ Example 5.4.1 (Strings over a three-letter alphabet) How many length- strings over use all three letters? Solution: We take to be all length- strings over the alphabet, so . We take , , and to be the strings missing the indicated letter. Each individual violation set has elements because only two letters remain available. If two specified letters are missing, every position must contain the one remaining letter, so each pairwise intersection has one element. No length- string can miss all three letters. Therefore the number using every letter is A length- string over an -symbol alphabet is the same object as a function from its set of positions to the alphabet. Using every symbol means that this function is surjective. Theorem 5.4.2 (Surjections) For finite sets and with and , and with the convention , the number of surjective functions is Proof First, we assume . The universe consists of all functions from to . For each , we take to be the functions whose images omit . If we omit specified elements of , each input has possible images, so the intersection contains functions. We choose the omitted values in ways and use the complement form of inclusion–exclusion. If , the empty function from to is surjective, while no function from a nonempty set to exists. The formula gives in the first case and in the second because of the convention . When , a surjection is impossible. The alternating sum in the theorem is then zero. The pigeonhole principle makes that conclusion visible even though the alternating sum hides the cancellation."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/05-inclusion-exclusion-and-applications","pageTitle":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications","heading":"5.5 Application: avoiding specified substrings","anchor":"l6:substrings","text":"5.5 Application: avoiding specified substrings In a permutation of distinct letters, we can sometimes count the condition that a specified word appears consecutively by gluing that word into one block. Inclusion–exclusion then combines several forbidden blocks. Example 5.5.1 (Avoiding three two-letter blocks) How many permutations of the letters in MODERNIST contain none of the substrings DO, RE, or MI? Solution: The nine letters are distinct, so the universe has permutations. We define To count , we treat DO as one object together with the other seven letters. We can arrange these eight objects in ways. The same count holds for each individual violation. The three prescribed blocks use disjoint letters. If two specified blocks must appear, we glue both of them and can arrange the resulting seven objects in ways. If all three appear, the three blocks and the remaining three letters form six objects, giving arrangements. Therefore the number containing none of the forbidden substrings is Warning 5.5.2 (Blocks can interact) The gluing count above works cleanly because the three substrings use disjoint letters and have fixed internal order. If forbidden substrings overlap, share letters, or can occur more than once, an intersection may have several possible overlap patterns. Inclusion–exclusion still applies, but each intersection requires an accurate model before we insert it into the formula."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/05-inclusion-exclusion-and-applications","pageTitle":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications","heading":"5.6 Application: derangements","anchor":"l6:derangements","text":"5.6 Application: derangements Inclusion–exclusion is particularly useful when a permutation must avoid specified positions. We write a permutation of as a function . An index is a fixed point if . Definition 5.6.1 (Derangement) A derangement is a permutation with no fixed points. We denote the number of derangements of by . Equivalently, if owners receive labeled hats, phones, or exams, a derangement is an assignment in which nobody receives their own object. Theorem 5.6.2 (Derangement formula) For every nonnegative integer , Proof We take to be the set of all permutations of . For each , we define the violation set A derangement belongs to none of these sets. If has size , then every permutation in fixes the specified elements. The remaining elements can appear in any order, so There are choices for . Applying the complement form of inclusion–exclusion gives the first formula. Since factoring out gives the second. The first few values are The convention records the one empty permutation and makes later formulas work without an exceptional case. Example 5.6.3 (Returning six phones) Six students place their labeled phones in a box, and the phones are returned in an arbitrary order. The number of assignments in which every student receives the wrong phone is To count assignments in which exactly two students receive their own phones, we first choose those two students and then derange the remaining four phones. The number is The same two-stage argument proves a useful refinement. Corollary 5.6.4 (Exactly fixed points) The number of permutations of with exactly fixed points is Proof We choose the fixed elements. The remaining elements must be deranged; otherwise there would be another fixed point. The choice of fixed elements and the derangement determine the permutation uniquely. For instance, no permutation has exactly fixed points: if all but one element are fixed, the remaining element also has nowhere else to go. The formula agrees because . Dividing the derangement formula by gives the probability that a uniformly random permutation is a derangement: These partial sums approach Thus, for large , the probability that nobody receives their own object is close to . The expression is an approximation, not the exact integer count . Problem (Practice) Eight exams are returned randomly to eight students. What are exact expressions for the number of returns in which no student receives their own exam; exactly three students receive their own exams; at least one student receives their own exam."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/05-inclusion-exclusion-and-applications","pageTitle":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications","heading":"5.7 Choosing useful violation sets","anchor":"l6:choosing-violations","text":"5.7 Choosing useful violation sets The four applications look different, but their solutions have the same structure. For bounded solutions, violation is ; shifting variables and using stars and bars counts an intersection. For all-symbol strings or surjections, violation is that symbol is missing; reducing the alphabet counts an intersection. For forbidden substrings, violation is that a specified block occurs, and gluing all required blocks counts an intersection once we understand their interactions. For derangements, violation is that position is fixed, and an fixing specified positions and permuting the rest counts an intersection. A productive violation set has two properties: the valid outcomes are precisely those with no violations, and intersections of specified violations are easier to count than the valid outcomes directly. If the intersections become harder than the original problem, a different universe or set of conditions may work better."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/05-inclusion-exclusion-and-applications","pageTitle":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications","heading":"5.8 Common errors and a final checklist","anchor":"l6:pie-checklist","text":"5.8 Common errors and a final checklist The universe. All violation sets must be subsets of the same clearly specified set . Precise events. For an upper bound , the violation is , not . A union versus its complement. Inclusion–exclusion starting with counts at least one violation. Starting with counts no violations. Simultaneous violations. A -fold intersection imposes all conditions at once; it is not generally the product of the sizes of the individual violation sets. The number of intersections. If every -fold intersection has a common size, we multiply it by . Impossible terms. An empty intersection contributes zero, so we do not extend the alternating sum with meaningless binomial coefficients. The reusable complement formula is The notation is compact, but a complete solution still explains what the universe, violations, and intersections mean for the concrete objects being counted."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/05-inclusion-exclusion-and-applications","pageTitle":"5. Inclusion–exclusion and applications","heading":"5.9 Further reading","anchor":"further-reading","text":"5.9 Further reading The course resources page links to the books used as references for these notes. Applied Combinatorics, Sections 7.1–7.4, covers the inclusion–exclusion formula, surjections, and derangements. Discrete Mathematics: An Open Introduction, Sections 3.3 and 3.8, covers overlap bookkeeping, bounded multisets, onto functions, and derangements. Counting Rocks!, Section 2.7, presents the first inclusion–exclusion cases."},{"pageId":"lecture-notes/unit-1-enumeration/index","pageTitle":"Unit I: Enumeration and inclusion-exclusion","heading":"Unit I: Enumeration and inclusion-exclusion","anchor":"unit-i-enumeration-and-inclusion-exclusion","text":"Unit I: Enumeration and inclusion-exclusion Unit I develops precise counting models and the basic rules for combining, subtracting, and correcting counts."},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Access and well-being","anchor":"access-and-well-being","text":"Access and well-being Center for Educational Access Counseling and Psychological Services U of A Cares University emergency information RazALERT"},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Additional references","anchor":"additional-references","text":"Additional references Eric Lehman, F. Thomson Leighton, and Albert R. Meyer, Mathematics for Computer Science, open PDF. Oscar Levin, Discrete Mathematics: An Open Introduction, open edition. Kenneth Bogart, Combinatorics Through Guided Discovery, open edition. Joy Morris, Combinatorics, version 2.1, open textbook. Henry Adams, Kelly Emmrich, Maria Gillespie, Shannon Golden, and Rachel Pries, Counting Rocks! An Introduction to Combinatorics, PDF."},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Main textbook","anchor":"main-textbook","text":"Main textbook M. T. Keller and W. T. Trotter, Applied Combinatorics: web edition and PDF edition."},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Mathematics help","anchor":"mathematics-help","text":"Mathematics help Mathematics Resource and Teaching Center Department tutor request Student Success academic initiatives"},{"pageId":"resources","pageTitle":"Resources","heading":"Resources","anchor":"resources","text":"Resources I will provide lecture notes or detailed references for all topics we cover. I may also include optional videos and other additional material as alternative explanations or extra practice."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Academic integrity","anchor":"academic-integrity","text":"Academic integrity We all share responsibility for creating a course environment built on honest work and learning. Follow the University of Arkansas Academic Integrity Policy. Specific expectations for collaboration and artificial intelligence appear on the assignments page."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Accessibility and accommodations","anchor":"accessibility-and-accommodations","text":"Accessibility and accommodations If you have approved accommodations, request your accommodation letter and contact me early so we can make arrangements in time. If you think you may need accommodations but have not set them up, the Center for Educational Access can help. Temporary conditions can affect access too, so reach out promptly when they arise."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Assessment","anchor":"assessment","text":"Assessment Component Weight Written hand-in homework 20% Online questions 15% Exam 1 16% Exam 2 16% Exam 3 16% Exam 4 16% Course check-in completion 1% Total 100% Grade weights breakdownWritten hand-in homework 20%; Online questions 15%; Exam 1 16%; Exam 2 16%; Exam 3 16%; Exam 4 16%; Course check-in completion 1%.Written hand-in homework: 20%Online questions: 15%Exam 1: 16%Exam 2: 16%Exam 3: 16%Exam 4: 16%Course check-in completion: 1%Written hand-in homework 20%Online questions 15%Exam 1 16%Exam 2 16%Exam 3 16%Exam 4 16%Course check-in completion 1%Course check-ins are brief questions I create to learn what is working for you in the course organization, pacing, and resources."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Attendance and participation","anchor":"attendance-and-participation","text":"Attendance and participation Attendance is not graded, and there is no absence penalty. If you need to miss class, use the posted notes, online questions, homework, and other resources to keep up independently. Please keep track of course material, announcements, deadlines, and assessments. If your work suggests that your current study routine is not serving you, I may reach out with ideas such as regular attendance or office hours. Please plan to be present for scheduled exams. I will not be able to offer make- up exams except for excused emergencies."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Communication, records, and course changes","anchor":"communication-records-and-course-changes","text":"Communication, records, and course changes Email is the best way to reach me with an individual question. I will post course-wide announcements, grades, and updates in Blackboard, so check your University email and Blackboard regularly. If you notice a possible grading or recording error, let me know promptly, within one week of the posted score. I may revise the syllabus when needed. If a change materially affects grading, exams, deadlines, or course delivery, I will announce it in writing. The current version of this website is our course reference."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Course organization","anchor":"course-organization","text":"Course organization In class, I will introduce new topics and talk through their applications with you. After most meetings, online questions provide a quick review of basic skills; they are graded automatically, and most allow multiple attempts. I will assign hand-in homework weekly. We will have three midterms and a final-period exam."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Course overview","anchor":"course-overview","text":"Course overview See the University of Arkansas mathematics course listing. Prerequisites are MATH 26103 or MATH 28003; the pre- or corequisite is MATH 30803 or MATH 30903. You should be comfortable working with sets, functions, relations, basic proofs and mathematical arguments, algebraic manipulation, and polynomials. Combinatorics begins with a simple question: How many ways can we arrange a collection of objects? Can a schedule, pairing, or design be made at all? What changes when two arrangements should count as the same? These questions show up in mathematics, computer science, probability, and more. We will learn foundational combinatorial techniques and the inclusion-exclusion principle, which allow us to count efficiently. We will then learn generating functions as algebraic bookkeeping for combinatorial quantities, followed by recurrence relations that model how later states depend on previous ones. Additional topics may include modular arithmetic and applications to cryptography, coding theory, graph theory, counting arrangements with symmetries, and elementary algebraic structures. Unit Main capability Enumeration and inclusion-exclusion Define objects precisely and count them using cases, bijections, double counting, and overlapping restrictions. Generating-function foundations Encode sequences as formal power series and interpret algebraic operations as counting operations. Recurrence models Select states and initial conditions, derive and solve recurrences, and compare recursive, memoized, and bottom-up evaluation. Further topics To be announced."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Dropped assignments","anchor":"dropped-assignments","text":"Dropped assignments The worst two hand-in homework assignments are dropped. The worst four online-question assignments are dropped. One extra online question is dropped if you complete all check-in work."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Inclement weather and emergencies","anchor":"inclement-weather-and-emergencies","text":"Inclement weather and emergencies We will follow University closure and inclement-weather decisions. If a closure prevents an exam, I will announce a new date through Blackboard and the course website. Please do not travel when conditions are unsafe. University emergency information RazALERT emergency notifications Academic Policy 1858.10: inclement weather"},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Learning outcomes","anchor":"learning-outcomes","text":"Learning outcomes By the end of the course, you should be able to: Use basic counting techniques, including permutations, combinations with repetition, and careful accounting for overcounting. Analyze a discrete model by identifying its objects, states, parameters, and constraints. Choose and switch representations to reveal a model’s useful structure. Explain a combinatorial argument clearly: describe the model, choose a representation, and apply the right known techniques. Describe a recursive model and analyze its behavior by identifying state information, a recurrence, and initial conditions, and by finding a closed form when possible. Use generating functions as formal algebraic models and extract the information they encode. Work with selected advanced discrete systems, which may include symmetry, graphs, algorithms, and complexity analysis."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Letter grades","anchor":"letter-grades","text":"Letter grades The following tentative distribution is a reference; I will let you know if it needs to change during the course. Course percentage Letter grade 88–100 A 76–87.99 B 64–75.99 C 51–63.99 D Below 51 F"},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Materials","anchor":"materials","text":"Materials Our lecture notes are the main guide through the course. The principal reference is Keller and Trotter, Applied Combinatorics: web edition and PDF edition. Additional references are listed on the resources page; you do not need to purchase any of the listed open materials."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Online questions","anchor":"online-questions","text":"Online questions Online questions normally follow a lecture and are due by the start of the next class meeting. They focus on comfort with key vocabulary and formulas."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Other information","anchor":"other-information","text":"Other information"},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Religious observances, University activities, and other excusable conflicts","anchor":"religious-observances-university-activities-and-other-excusable-conflicts","text":"Religious observances, University activities, and other excusable conflicts Email me as early as you can about religious observances, required University activities, military service, jury duty, documented illness, a family crisis, or another excusable conflict. For an exam conflict you can anticipate, letting me know at least one week ahead is especially helpful. We will follow applicable University policies and deadlines. If you have three or more final exams on one day, you may be able to reschedule one under Academic Policy 1500.20. If it involves this course, request it before the Registrar’s withdrawal deadline."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Student support","anchor":"student-support","text":"Student support Mathematics Resource and Teaching Center Student Success Counseling and Psychological Services U of A Cares Additional links are collected on the resources page."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Syllabus","anchor":"syllabus","text":"Syllabus Course Combinatorics MATH 31003-01 Semester Fall 2026 Instructor Gennady Uraltsev Email uraltsev@uark.edu Office SCEN 421 Class meetings Monday, Wednesday, Friday 8:35-9:25 a.m., GEAR 108 Office hours Wednesday 4:00-5:00 p.m., SCEN 421"},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Unit exams","anchor":"unit-exams","text":"Unit exams There are four unit exams: three in-class exams and one during the first part of the final-exam period. Every exam problem will be parallel to an assigned homework problem in its required method and main solution steps, though the parameters, objects, or wording may change. Exam Date or period Scope Exam 1 Friday, September 18 To be announced Exam 2 Friday, October 16 To be announced Exam 3 Friday, November 13 To be announced Exam 4 First hour of the Registrar-assigned final exam To be announced Mastery reassessment Second part of the same final period One earlier midterm unit selected by the student During the second part of the final exam period, you may choose to reassess your mastery of one of exam units 1–3. The reassessment covers the same unit and methods. If the reassessment score is higher, it replaces the earlier score; if it is lower, the earlier score remains. For each exam, you may use one standard letter- or A4-size page of handwritten notes that you prepared yourself. If you need an alternate or accommodated testing arrangement, contact me and the appropriate University office at least two weeks in advance."},{"pageId":"syllabus","pageTitle":"Syllabus","heading":"Weekly hand-in homework","anchor":"weekly-hand-in-homework","text":"Weekly hand-in homework A typical assignment has several problems and may involve computation, modeling, proofs, or other mathematical arguments. Submit it online as a PDF; a handwritten, scanned PDF is welcome. Make sure it is legible, labels each problem, explains the method and final answer, and uses complete mathematical statements. Follow the deadline listed on the assignment page. If you need more time, talk to me in advance of the deadline so we can work out an extension. Unforeseen emergencies are handled case by case."}];
